{
  "content": "**Updated conversation-analysis cheatsheet**\n\n### 1) Reconstruct the active user spec as a living constraint set\nStart from the latest user turn, then merge in earlier user constraints only if they still survive. Treat every new user message as potentially subtracting, tightening, or replacing prior requirements. The analyzer should compare the assistant against this resolved spec, not against earlier paraphrases, assistant interpretations, or the broader topic of the thread.\n\n### 2) Let the latest user turn override earlier assumptions\nWhen the user narrows the request, shifts the output type, or adds a new constraint, re-evaluate from that point forward. Older broader requirements become irrelevant unless the user explicitly keeps them. Do not carry forward overproduction or outdated scope from earlier turns if the latest user wording has made them obsolete.\n\n### 3) Separate task intent from output shape\nCheck what the user wants selected, filtered, excluded, or related to separately from what the user wants returned. A response can be semantically on-target yet still diverge by giving the wrong granularity, projection, cardinality, grouping, ordering, or presentation. This is especially important when the user asks for a minimal result or a narrow field set.\n\n### 4) Treat additive refinements as deltas, not rewrites\nWhen the user says \u201calso,\u201d \u201cinclude,\u201d \u201cplus,\u201d or adds a new attribute, diagnose the change as an incremental update to the existing task. Preserve the aligned core and isolate what the assistant missed in the new delta. Avoid replacing a mostly correct answer with a generic \u201cwrong\u201d judgment when the real issue is a single missing constraint.\n\n### 5) Bias toward subtraction when the user\u2019s wording gets narrower\nIf later user turns reduce the scope, the analyzer should actively look for requirements that have been superseded and should no longer be present. Watch for \u201conly,\u201d \u201cjust,\u201d \u201cIDs only,\u201d \u201cnames only,\u201d \u201cwithout,\u201d \u201cno,\u201d or similar narrowing language. A later narrow request often means extra fields, counts, joins, or explanations now count as divergence even if they were earlier acceptable.\n\n### 6) Diagnose extra structure as a mismatch, not a harmless enhancement\nUnrequested sorting, aliases, helper columns, counts, lists, aggregations, deduplication, joins, or explanatory text can all change the answer\u2019s shape. If the user did not ask for that structure, treat it as overgeneration rather than improvement. This matters even when the core entity or relation is correct.\n\n### 7) Check each constraint dimension independently\nEvaluate filters, exclusions, relation path, selected fields, grouping, ordering, and granularity as separate dimensions. Do not collapse them into one generic correctness call. A response can be right on one axis and wrong on another, and the analysis should say exactly which axis diverged.\n\n### 8) Distinguish relationship-preserving matches from mere relatedness\nIf the user asks for something associated with, linked to, carried out by, or otherwise relational, verify that the assistant preserves the intended relationship rather than returning a loosely related entity. The key question is whether the referential path the user asked for is still intact. Dropping that path is a spec miss even if the topic feels adjacent.\n\n### 9) Use the user\u2019s exact wording to resolve ambiguity\nWhen the user names an entity, field, or population directly, that wording is the strongest evidence for the intended constraint. Prefer the user\u2019s phrasing over richer but unsupported interpretations. Concrete examples and exact terms should resolve ambiguity toward the narrowest faithful reading, not the most elaborate one.\n\n### 10) Separate confirmed requirements from speculative additions\nDo not promote inferred defaults, plausible schema assumptions, or defensive extras into user intent. If the user never asked for a field, safeguard, table, or transformation, treat it as optional or speculative. This prevents the analyzer from inflating the spec and then judging the assistant against requirements the user never gave.\n\n### 11) Watch for inversions in user language\nPhrases like \u201cdidn\u2019t,\u201d \u201cno,\u201d \u201cnone,\u201d \u201cwithout,\u201d or \u201cexcluding\u201d often flip the task from inclusion to exclusion. These are major spec changes, not minor wording tweaks. The analyzer should confirm whether the assistant switched from positive matching to exclusion logic, rather than assuming an earlier inclusion-based answer still applies.\n\n### 12) Distinguish partial alignment from final alignment\nKeep the correct core logic in view, but verify whether later refinements introduced omissions or extra content. Earlier alignment does not excuse a final answer that misses the latest user constraints. The final judgment should reflect the full surviving spec, not the best earlier partial match.\n\n### 13) Prefer minimal issue reports grounded in user evidence\nWhen diagnosing divergence, state exactly what is missing, extra, or reshaped relative to the user\u2019s request. Avoid vague quality feedback or implementation advice unless the mismatch depends on it. The best report is specific enough that a later response can correct the exact delta.\n\n### 14) Keep the analysis compact and transferable\nRetain only the active user-derived spec and the directly relevant aligned reasoning. Drop assistant-side detours, speculative branches, and outdated subtasks so later turns are not anchored to irrelevant alternatives. The ideal analysis is the smallest one that still explains the mismatch clearly and consistently.\n\n### 15) In incremental threads, treat convergence as possible and do not invent regressions\nIf the assistant already satisfies the fully accumulated user request, do not flag issues from earlier turns that the user has since superseded. Likewise, do not mark a conversation as divergent merely because earlier versions were broader if the final response matches the current narrowed spec. The analyzer should recognize when the conversation has effectively converged.",
  "version": 5,
  "history": [
    "",
    "**Updated conversation-analysis cheatsheet**\n\n### 1) Build the task spec cumulatively from user statements only\nTreat each new user turn as a constraint update, not a fresh task. Maintain a running spec ledger of all user-stated requirements, and update it with later refinements. Earlier assistant-generated interpretations, option lists, and speculative branches do not count as settled requirements unless the user confirms them. If a later user turn narrows or clarifies the request, that newer constraint overrides older inferred interpretations.\n\n### 2) Re-evaluate against the latest user constraint, not the earlier conversation state\nThe analyzer should always compare the assistant\u2019s latest action to the most recent user message plus any still-valid prior user constraints. Do not freeze the task after an intermediate interpretation. If the user has refined the request, re-check whether prior assumptions still hold and whether the final response matches the updated target.\n\n### 3) Distinguish explicit requirements from provisional assumptions\nOnly user-stated output fields, filters, populations, ranking criteria, or deliverable formats belong in the confirmed spec. If something is merely plausible based on the conversation, keep it provisional rather than silently treating it as required. This is especially important when the user has not resolved tie-breaking, exact granularity, or whether auxiliary attributes are needed.\n\n### 4) Watch for scope narrowing and flag scope expansion\nPhrases like \u201cfocus on X\u201d usually narrow the output scope, not expand it into a menu of possibilities. If the assistant responds by enumerating multiple sub-asks, alternative outputs, or analysis branches that the user did not choose, treat that as scope expansion. The analyzer should prefer the narrowest faithful task spec and flag extra fields, joins, variants, or explanatory digressions that are no longer necessary.\n\n### 5) Use the latest user message to resolve ambiguity, but do not over-commit\nWhen the conversation is still evolving, avoid locking into one interpretation of a partially specified query. Instead, track what the user has actually fixed and what remains open. If a later user turn makes the target effectively determined, stop privileging clarification-seeking branches; repeated questioning in that state is an anchoring risk, not good caution.\n\n### 6) Track output shape separately from conceptual intent\nA response can be conceptually aligned while still failing on deliverable shape. Check whether the assistant produced the exact required artifact type and granularity: entity only, entity plus attributes, ranked row, count only, or a single final answer versus a bundle of options. Mismatches in answer shape are often the real divergence, even when the underlying logic seems close.\n\n### 7) Treat incremental edits as deltas to the full required output\nWhen the user adds a field or constraint, verify the response against the full accumulated set, not just the newest addition. Repeated mention of an already-satisfied field is usually a no-op; do not over-interpret it as a new requirement unless it changes the output. The analyzer should distinguish \u201cpartially correct\u201d from \u201ccomplete,\u201d and name the exact missing or extra element.\n\n### 8) Compare the assistant\u2019s final answer to the final spec, not to earlier assistant reasoning\nThe assistant may \u201cfix\u201d one issue while introducing another. The analyzer should focus on whether the final answer satisfies the latest user constraints after all refinements, not whether it matches an earlier assistant-generated interpretation. When compacting context, keep the stable core intent that survives refinements and drop obsolete branches so earlier false leads do not anchor later judgments.\n\n### 9) Flag over-generation and unnecessary structure\nIf the user asks for one direct result, then extra variants, parameterized alternatives, grouped summaries, or explanatory prefaces are mismatches. Multiple related outputs can be individually reasonable but still diverge from a narrowly specified ask. Prefer direct execution over analysis framing once the task is settled.\n\n### 10) Pay attention to semantic refinements in constraints\nSmall wording changes can materially alter the task. The analyzer should detect when a refinement changes the population being considered, the inclusion/exclusion rule, or the ranking criterion rather than treating it as a minor paraphrase. In particular, do not collapse \u201call items\u201d into \u201citems that satisfy an additional property\u201d when the user\u2019s latest wording changes that boundary.\n\n### 11) Separate conceptual alignment from implementation alignment\nA solution can match the intended logic but still be wrong in concrete details such as schema, grouping, join direction, or other operational choices. These implementation mismatches matter when they change the actual result set. However, if a lower-level issue is secondary and the user did not depend on exact mechanics, avoid overstating it as the primary divergence.\n\n### 12) Be alert to null-result and inclusion/exclusion semantics\nWhen the user emphasizes that results must have truly occurred, truly existed, or otherwise be non-empty in reality, suspect mistakes that would allow empty matches or irrelevant placeholders. The analyzer should treat inclusion semantics carefully when the request implies actual occurrence rather than potential association.\n\n### 13) Keep the issue report concrete and requirement-based\nPrefer naming the exact user-stated requirement that remains unsatisfied over broad quality critiques. The most useful divergence reports say what changed, what is missing, or what extra content appeared relative to the user\u2019s spec. Avoid vague \u201cbe more careful\u201d feedback unless no concrete mismatch can be identified.\n\n### 14) In multi-turn refinement, re-run the check from scratch each time\nEven if the assistant was correct in prior turns, the latest user update can invalidate the prior fit. The analyzer should not carry forward a presumption of correctness across refinements. Re-evaluate the final response against the updated ledger each time, using the current turn as the source of truth for what now matters.",
    "**Updated conversation-analysis cheatsheet**\n\n### 1) Build and maintain a running spec from user messages only\nTreat each user turn as a delta on the task spec, not a reset. Add newly stated constraints, drop constraints the user has clearly overridden, and carry forward only what remains valid. Do not let assistant-generated interpretations, speculative branches, or intermediate drafts become part of the confirmed spec unless the user explicitly confirms them.\n\n### 2) Re-parse the latest user turn for scope shifts before judging\nThe latest user message may narrow, refine, or redirect the task. Re-evaluate the conversation against the newest user intent plus surviving prior constraints, not against an earlier broad interpretation. If the user has advanced from an intermediate subtask to a final ask, flag answers that are still solving the earlier step as incomplete or mis-scoped.\n\n### 3) Separate task intent from output shape\nA response can be logically related to the ask yet still diverge in deliverable form. Track whether the user wants a single result, a filtered list, grouped aggregates, row-level detail, a specific field set, or a compact summary. Mismatches in granularity, cardinality, or field inclusion are often the real error, even when the underlying reasoning looks plausible.\n\n### 4) Prefer the narrowest faithful spec\nWhen the user refines with phrases like \u201cfocus on,\u201d \u201cspecifically,\u201d \u201cinclude,\u201d or \u201ccheck for,\u201d treat that as a tightening of scope unless the wording clearly expands it. Avoid turning one requested target into a menu of alternatives, extra summaries, or broader analysis branches. Extra columns, extra totals, or extra explanatory structure should be marked as drift unless the user asked for them.\n\n### 5) Distinguish explicit requirements from plausible but unconfirmed assumptions\nOnly user-stated fields, filters, populations, ranking criteria, and formatting constraints belong in the settled spec. If something is merely a reasonable guess from context, keep it provisional. Do not upgrade implementation choices, hygiene checks, or inferred matching logic into user requirements unless the user actually asked for them.\n\n### 6) Track incremental refinements as deltas to the full output\nWhen the user adds a field or constraint, verify the assistant against the full accumulated set, not only the newest addition. Repeated mention of an already-satisfied element is usually not a new requirement. The key question is whether the final answer is complete relative to the updated spec, not whether it improved in the latest turn.\n\n### 7) Detect partial satisfaction and missing refinements separately\nA response can preserve the right population or grouping while still missing the newest field-level or shape-level refinement. Evaluate filters, joins, aggregates, and output fields as separate dimensions. Report exactly what is missing, extra, or mis-shaped rather than collapsing everything into a generic \u201cincorrect\u201d label.\n\n### 8) Compare the final assistant answer to the final spec, not to earlier assistant reasoning\nThe assistant may correct one issue and introduce another, or preserve a useful structure while changing the wrong part. Judge the final answer against the latest user-defined target after all refinements, and ignore obsolete branches that the user has implicitly or explicitly dropped. Compact the context so stale interpretations do not anchor the analysis.\n\n### 9) Treat over-generation as a conversation-level error\nIf the user asks for one direct result, then multiple variants, broad digressions, or bundled explanations are mismatches. Even individually reasonable extras can violate a narrowly specified ask. Prefer direct execution over exploratory framing once the task is settled.\n\n### 10) Distinguish conceptual alignment from implementation alignment\nA response can match the intended logic but still fail in concrete details that change the result set or deliverable. Check for schema/structure mismatches, incorrect grouping level, wrong field placement, or other operational choices that alter what is returned. Only elevate lower-level issues when they materially affect the user\u2019s requested outcome.\n\n### 11) Use the user\u2019s exact wording to resolve ambiguity when possible\nIf the user names a field, entity, population, or inclusion/exclusion rule explicitly, treat that wording as the strongest evidence for the intended constraint. Pay attention to whether the wording implies exact match versus broader pattern matching, or whether it narrows a previous broader scope. Do not over-interpret similar-sounding constraints into broader exclusions or richer reporting than requested.\n\n### 12) Separate user requirements from assistant-added safeguards\nChecks like null guards, formatting cleanup, or defensive validation may be sensible, but they are not user intent unless explicitly requested. Do not penalize or praise them as if they were part of the task spec. Only surface them when they affect the actual result or when the user specifically required that behavior.\n\n### 13) When the request is fully determined, stop seeking clarification\nRepeated clarification-seeking after the user has already made the target clear is an anchoring problem, not prudence. Once the intent and output shape are sufficiently fixed by the conversation, the analyzer should treat the task as determined and evaluate the final answer directly. Preserve only the constraints that still matter.\n\n### 14) Keep the issue report concrete, requirement-based, and minimal\nState what changed, what is missing, or what is extra relative to the user\u2019s spec. Avoid vague quality critiques or stylistic complaints unless no concrete mismatch exists. The best corrective direction identifies the exact requirement gap while preserving any aligned structure that should be carried into the next response.",
    "**Updated conversation-analysis cheatsheet**\n\n### 1) Rebuild the task spec from user turns only, as a living contract\nTreat each user message as a constraint update on the current task, not a fresh reset. Add newly stated requirements, remove requirements the user clearly superseded, and keep only the constraints that remain compatible. Do not let assistant-generated interpretations, speculative branches, or interim drafts become part of the settled spec unless the user explicitly confirms them.\n\n### 2) Make the latest user turn the active priority, then reconcile earlier turns\nBefore judging the assistant, re-parse the newest user message for scope shifts, narrowing language, or changed output intent. The correct reference point is the latest complete user intent, plus any earlier constraints that still survive it. Avoid flagging an answer for omitting earlier requirements that the user has since dropped or replaced.\n\n### 3) Separate current intent from prior discussion and prior assistant reasoning\nThe analyzer should distinguish between what the user currently wants and what was previously discussed. Old assistant reasoning can be useful context, but it must not anchor the evaluation if the user later narrowed or redirected the task. Compare the final assistant answer against the final spec, not against earlier branches or earlier partial interpretations.\n\n### 4) Accumulate only stable constraints; discard stale carryover\nWhen user requirements evolve incrementally, carry forward only the parts that still fit the latest request. Watch for stale columns, filters, metrics, grouping, or narrative branches that survive from older turns but no longer belong. This is especially important when the user says \u201conly,\u201d \u201calso,\u201d \u201cinclude,\u201d \u201cfocus on,\u201d or otherwise tightens scope.\n\n### 5) Diagnose scope shrinkage aggressively and treat overproduction as divergence\nIf the user narrows the request to a minimal result, extra detail is usually an error even if it is useful. Additional rows, summaries, counts, IDs, explanations, joins, or derived fields can all be mismatches when they exceed the requested output shape. Treat unnecessary broadening as a conversation-level issue, not a harmless enhancement.\n\n### 6) Evaluate output shape, grain, and presentation independently from content correctness\nA response can target the right subject while still failing on deliverable form. Check row-level versus summary-level output, single result versus list, field inclusion, grouping level, sorting order, and cardinality as separate dimensions. In structured tasks, a query can be semantically close yet still wrong if it returns the wrong grain or shape.\n\n### 7) Distinguish user-stated requirements from plausible but unconfirmed assumptions\nOnly user-provided constraints belong in the final spec: explicit fields, populations, exclusion rules, ordering keys, and formatting requirements. Do not upgrade reasonable guesses from schema, context, or domain knowledge into user intent. If something is merely inferred, keep it provisional or unresolved rather than treating it as required.\n\n### 8) Use the user\u2019s exact wording to resolve ambiguity when possible\nWhen the user names a field, entity, population, or rule directly, that wording is the strongest evidence for the intended constraint. Exact phrasing often resolves whether a request is narrow or broad, exact-match or approximate, inclusive or exclusive. Prefer the user\u2019s wording over a richer interpretation that adds extra meaning.\n\n### 9) Check each constraint dimension separately: filters, grouping, ordering, and output fields\nDo not assume that satisfying one dimension implies the others are also correct. A response can preserve the right population while missing the newest field, or have the right fields but the wrong sort key. Report the specific mismatch rather than collapsing everything into a generic correctness judgment.\n\n### 10) Detect unnecessary structural changes, especially in structured outputs\nIf the assistant introduces grouping, deduplication, joins, aggregation, or other structural transformations not demanded by the user, treat that as potential drift. Even a \u201chelpful\u201d rewrite can change the result set or the information delivered. Flag changes that alter the task\u2019s output shape unless the user explicitly asked for them.\n\n### 11) Prefer the minimal faithful spec and minimal necessary issue report\nWhen the request is fully determined, stop seeking clarification and evaluate directly. When reporting problems, state only what is missing, extra, or mis-shaped relative to the user\u2019s spec. The best corrective direction is concrete and requirement-based, not a vague quality critique or a suggestion to \u201cimprove\u201d the answer.\n\n### 12) Separate missing support from unsupported speculation\nIf the assistant asserts a concept that the conversation or available context does not establish, do not accept it as fulfilled. Mark it as unresolved or speculative rather than confirmed. Likewise, if the assistant adds defensive checks, validation, or other safeguards not requested by the user, do not count them as part of the task unless they affect the requested result.\n\n### 13) Preserve aligned parts, but do not let them mask a later mismatch\nA response may contain a useful structure, a correct join path, or a partially correct filter while still failing on a later refinement. Reuse the aligned pieces in analysis, but judge the final answer against the full updated spec. This prevents earlier correct fragments from hiding a newer omission or an incorrect narrowing/broadening.\n\n### 14) When user turns are cumulative, verify the final answer against the full surviving constraint set\nThe right question is whether the final answer satisfies all remaining user requirements together. Repeated mention of an already-satisfied element usually does not add a new requirement. Focus on what changed in the latest turn, then test the final response against the whole resolved spec, not just the newest delta.\n\n### 15) Keep the analysis concrete, minimal, and transferable\nState the exact requirement gap: what was omitted, what was added, or what was transformed incorrectly. Avoid stylistic complaints, speculative criticism, or implementation advice unless the concrete mismatch depends on them. The analyzer\u2019s job is to identify divergence from user intent, not to prescribe a solution method.",
    "**Updated conversation-analysis cheatsheet**\n\n### 1) Rebuild the task spec from user turns only, and treat it as cumulative but editable\nExtract the user\u2019s current request from the latest turn first, then merge it with earlier user constraints that still remain active. If a later user message narrows, restates, or changes the task, update the spec accordingly instead of anchoring on earlier paraphrases or exploratory branches. Only user-provided constraints belong in the final spec; assistant-generated interpretations stay provisional unless the user confirms them.\n\n### 2) Make the latest user turn the active constraint update\nBefore judging the assistant, re-parse the newest user message for scope shifts, tighter wording, changed output intent, or an added metric/attribute. The analyzer should test whether the assistant already satisfies that narrower request; if not, mark the exact missing constraint rather than concluding the answer is broadly fine. Avoid flagging omissions from older turns when the user has since replaced or superseded them.\n\n### 3) Compare the assistant against the resolved user spec, not the conversation topic\nSeparate the current user intent from the broader discussion. The final answer should be evaluated against the surviving constraint set, not against earlier branches, prior assistant reasoning, or the general subject of the thread. Useful prior context can help resolve references, but it must not override the latest user wording.\n\n### 4) Track output shape independently from semantic correctness\nA response can identify the right subject or relation while still failing on grain, cardinality, grouping, field set, ordering, or presentation. Check row-level versus summary-level output, one item versus many, and requested columns versus extra columns as separate dimensions. In structured tasks, correct logic is not enough if the final shape diverges from what the user asked for.\n\n### 5) Treat overproduction as divergence when the user\u2019s request is narrow\nIf the user asks for a minimal or specific output, extra rows, extra fields, joins, explanations, or derived details are usually mismatches even when they are helpful. The analyzer should diagnose unnecessary broadening as a conversation-level error, not a harmless enhancement. When the user tightens scope, the minimal faithful answer is often the right benchmark.\n\n### 6) Diagnose additive refinements as deltas, not rewrites\nWhen a user says \u201calso,\u201d \u201cinclude,\u201d or adds another attribute/metric, treat that as an incremental update to the same task, not permission to expand the solution structure. Check whether the assistant preserved the aligned core and only missed the new requirement, versus drifting into a different result shape. This helps isolate the exact delta instead of replacing a mostly correct answer.\n\n### 7) Separate schema-valid reasoning from spec alignment\nA query or response can be internally valid, plausible, or well-formed and still miss the user\u2019s exact request. Distinguish whether the assistant found the right record/relation from whether it returned the right columns, level of detail, or final presentation. Do not let technical correctness conceal a mismatch in user intent.\n\n### 8) Treat extra structure as a potential mismatch, especially in structured outputs\nWatch for unnecessary grouping, aggregation, deduplication, joins, sorting, or other transformations that were not demanded by the user. Even a helpful structural change can alter what information is returned. Flag these changes when they shift the output\u2019s grain or content beyond the user\u2019s stated needs.\n\n### 9) Use exact user wording to resolve ambiguity whenever possible\nWhen the user names an entity, field, population, or relationship directly, that phrasing is the strongest evidence for the intended constraint. Exact wording often determines whether the request is narrow or broad, exact-match or approximate, or relation-preserving versus merely related. Prefer the user\u2019s wording over a richer interpretation that adds unsupported meaning.\n\n### 10) Distinguish relationship-preserving references from mere relatedness\nWhen the user asks for something \u201cassociated with,\u201d \u201clinked to,\u201d or otherwise relational, verify that the assistant preserves the relationship the user asked for rather than substituting a loosely related entity. The key question is whether the output keeps the intended referential path, not just whether the topic is adjacent. If the relationship is part of the request, dropping it is a spec miss.\n\n### 11) Check each constraint dimension separately\nTest filters, grouping, ordering, requested fields, relation path, and granularity independently. A response can be right on one dimension and wrong on another, so avoid collapsing all problems into a generic correctness judgment. Report the specific divergence so the issue is actionable.\n\n### 12) Preserve aligned parts, but do not let them mask a later mismatch\nKeep any correct core logic, valid join path, or right entity reference in view, but verify whether later refinements introduced omissions or overbroad additions. Earlier alignment does not excuse a final answer that misses the latest constraint set. The final judgment should reflect the full surviving spec.\n\n### 13) Separate confirmed requirements from speculative assumptions\nDo not upgrade inferred schema details, plausible defaults, or defensive additions into user intent. If something is not explicitly supported by the user\u2019s messages, treat it as optional, unresolved, or speculative rather than required. This is especially important when the assistant introduces unsupported columns, tables, or safeguards.\n\n### 14) Prefer minimal, concrete issue reports grounded in user evidence\nWhen something is wrong, say exactly what is missing, extra, or reshaped relative to the user\u2019s request. Avoid vague quality feedback, stylistic critiques, or implementation advice unless the mismatch depends on them. The analyzer\u2019s job is to identify divergence from user intent, not to prescribe a solution method.\n\n### 15) Keep the analysis compact and transferable\nRetain only the user-derived spec and the directly relevant aligned reasoning. Drop assistant-side detours and speculative branches so later turns are not anchored to irrelevant alternatives. The best analysis is the smallest one that still explains the mismatch clearly and consistently."
